Yes. Below is a **Moodle-style Single Answer MCQ practice set** based directly on the uploaded **Physical Organization of Parallel Platforms – Part 2** lesson. Each question has **ONE correct answer only**. 

## ICA MCQ Practice — Set 1

**Instructions:** Select **ONE** answer for each question.

### 1. What does PRAM stand for?

A. Parallel Random Access Machine
B. Processor Random Access Memory
C. Parallel Remote Access Machine
D. Processor Read Access Machine

---

### 2. In an ideal PRAM model, processors access:

A. Separate memory spaces
B. A global shared memory
C. Only local cache
D. Only secondary storage

---

### 3. Which PRAM model allows neither concurrent reads nor concurrent writes?

A. CREW
B. CRCW
C. ERCW
D. EREW

---

### 4. Which PRAM model allows concurrent reads but exclusive writes?

A. EREW
B. CREW
C. ERCW
D. CRCW

---

### 5. Which PRAM model allows exclusive reads and concurrent writes?

A. ERCW
B. CREW
C. EREW
D. CRCW

---

### 6. Which PRAM model allows both concurrent reads and concurrent writes?

A. EREW
B. CREW
C. ERCW
D. CRCW

---

### 7. Which is the weakest PRAM model?

A. CRCW
B. ERCW
C. CREW
D. EREW

---

### 8. Which is the most powerful PRAM model?

A. EREW
B. CREW
C. ERCW
D. CRCW

---

### 9. In the Common concurrent-write protocol, a write is allowed when:

A. The highest-priority processor writes
B. All processors write identical values
C. The first processor writes
D. The sum of all values is written

---

### 10. In the Priority protocol, which processor succeeds?

A. Lowest-priority processor
B. Random processor
C. Highest-priority processor
D. Last processor

---

### 11. In the Sum protocol, what happens when several processors write values concurrently?

A. Only the first value is stored
B. The highest value is stored
C. The values are summed
D. All writes fail

---

### 12. An interconnection network provides mechanisms for:

A. Data transfer between processing nodes
B. Only data storage
C. Only instruction execution
D. Only cache replacement

---

### 13. A physical communication medium such as wires or fibers is called a:

A. Switch
B. Link
C. Router table
D. Cache

---

### 14. A static network is also called a:

A. Indirect network
B. Dynamic network
C. Direct network
D. Switching network

---

### 15. A dynamic network is also called a:

A. Direct network
B. Indirect network
C. Static network
D. Linear network

---

### 16. Which network uses communication links connected dynamically by switches?

A. Static network
B. Dynamic network
C. Linear array
D. Ring

---

### 17. Which of the following is a responsibility of a network interface?

A. Packetizing data
B. Increasing CPU clock speed
C. Managing the operating system
D. Compiling source code

---

### 18. Physical topology describes:

A. How data logically flows
B. Physical placement of network components
C. CPU instruction execution
D. Memory allocation

---

### 19. Logical topology describes:

A. Cable installation
B. Physical device location
C. How data flows through the network
D. Processor clock frequency

---

### 20. Nodal degree refers to:

A. Maximum distance between nodes
B. Number of links connected to a node
C. Number of processors in the network
D. Number of switches in the network

---

### 21. Network diameter is the:

A. Minimum number of links in the network
B. Maximum distance between any two processing nodes
C. Number of processors connected to a bus
D. Number of switches in one stage

---

### 22. Arc connectivity refers to the minimum number of ______ that must be removed to disconnect a network.

A. Processors
B. Memory modules
C. Arcs/links
D. Cache lines

---

### 23. Bisection width is the minimum number of communication links required to:

A. Connect two processors
B. Partition the network into two equal halves
C. Increase bandwidth
D. Add a new processor

---

### 24. What is the main characteristic of a bus-based network?

A. Every node has a private communication link
B. Communication wires are shared
C. Every node connects to every other node
D. It contains no communication medium

---

### 25. What is a major advantage of a bus-based network?

A. Very high cost
B. Low cost and flexibility
C. Unlimited bandwidth
D. No contention

---

### 26. What happens as more devices are connected to a shared bus?

A. Contention generally decreases
B. Performance always increases
C. Contention increases
D. Diameter becomes zero

---

### 27. What is the diameter of a bus-based network according to the lesson?

A. 0
B. 1
C. 2
D. p − 1

---

### 28. What is the nodal degree of a bus-based network?

A. 0
B. 1
C. 2
D. p − 1

---

### 29. Crossbar networks primarily use:

A. A linear chain of processors
B. A grid of switches
C. A single shared wire
D. A tree of caches

---

### 30. A crossbar network is considered:

A. Blocking
B. Non-blocking
C. Static only
D. Randomized

---

### 31. In a crossbar network, a conflict occurs when two processors:

A. Access different memory modules
B. Access the same memory module simultaneously
C. Execute different instructions
D. Use different processors

---

### 32. Compared with a bus, a crossbar network generally provides:

A. Lower performance
B. Higher performance
C. No simultaneous communication
D. No switching

---

### 33. What is a major disadvantage of a crossbar network?

A. Low performance
B. High switch and link cost
C. No simultaneous communication
D. No memory access

---

### 34. If a crossbar connects `p` processors to `b` memory banks, its number of switching nodes is:

A. Θ(p + b)
B. Θ(p − b)
C. Θ(pb)
D. Θ(p/b)

---

### 35. As the number of processors increases, the switching-network complexity of the crossbar grows as:

A. O(1)
B. Ω(p²)
C. O(log p)
D. Ω(p)

---

### 36. A multistage network is positioned between:

A. Ring and mesh
B. Bus and crossbar
C. Star and hypercube
D. Linear array and ring

---

### 37. Compared with a crossbar, a multistage network is generally more scalable in terms of:

A. Cost
B. Processor speed
C. Cache size
D. Memory capacity

---

### 38. An Omega network contains how many stages for `p` inputs?

A. p
B. p/2
C. log₂ p
D. 2p

---

### 39. Each stage of an Omega network contains:

A. p switches
B. p/2 switches
C. log₂p switches
D. 2p switches

---

### 40. The interconnection pattern used in an Omega network is called:

A. Binary tree
B. Perfect shuffle
C. Ring shuffle
D. Random shuffle

---

### 41. An Omega switch has which two connection modes?

A. Read and write
B. Input and output
C. Pass-through and cross-over
D. Static and dynamic

---

### 42. In Omega routing, if the relevant source and destination bits are the same, the switch uses:

A. Cross-over
B. Pass-through
C. Random routing
D. Broadcast

---

### 43. In Omega routing, if the relevant source and destination bits are different, the switch uses:

A. Pass-through
B. Cross-over
C. Broadcast
D. Invalidation

---

### 44. An Omega network is classified as a:

A. Non-blocking network
B. Blocking network
C. Fully connected network
D. Ring network

---

### 45. In a completely connected network, each node has a direct link to:

A. Only one node
B. Two nodes
C. Every other node
D. The central node only

---

### 46. What is the diameter of a completely connected network?

A. 0
B. 1
C. 2
D. p − 1

---

### 47. What is the nodal degree of a completely connected network containing `p` nodes?

A. 1
B. 2
C. p
D. p − 1

---

### 48. In a star-connected network, one processor acts as the:

A. Memory bank
B. Central processor
C. Cache controller
D. Router table

---

### 49. What is the main bottleneck in a star topology?

A. Edge processor
B. Central processor
C. Memory bank
D. Cache line

---

### 50. What is the diameter of a star-connected network?

A. 1
B. 2
C. p − 1
D. p/2

---

